clove PROTOCOL notes — the I2P BitTorrent dialect and SAM interop
=================================================================

OpenSSH keeps a PROTOCOL file recording where its implementation pins down
things the specs leave vague. This is ours. It doubles as the interop lab
notebook (SCOPE §9): every place the upstream spec is underspecified or a
library/router behaves surprisingly, the observation and clove's decision
land here. Entries are dated by milestone, not calendar.

Status legend:
  [decided]   clove behaves this way; verified in-tree or against a router.
  [assumed]   clove behaves this way; NOT yet verified against a live router.
  [open]      unresolved; needs a router or upstream clarification.


1. Addresses and peer identity
------------------------------

1.1 [decided] Peer identity is the 32-byte SHA-256 of a full I2P
    destination (the "destination hash"). This is the only peer identifier
    the engine handles; there is no IP/port anywhere (SCOPE §3).

1.2 [decided] Outbound dial: a peer known only by its 32-byte hash is dialed
    at "<b32>.b32.i2p", where <b32> is RFC 4648 base32 (lowercase, no
    padding) of the 32 hash bytes. 32 bytes -> 52 base32 chars. The router
    resolves the b32 to a full destination via its netDb.
    Implemented in i2pnet::addr, tested against RFC 4648 vectors.

    1.2a [decided] The decode is strict about the final character's spare
      bits. 52 base32 characters carry 260 bits and a destination hash is
      256, so a lax decoder maps sixteen distinct labels onto one identity
      ("…ljnj" and "…ljna" would name the same peer). Found by the mutation
      sweep in i2pnet::addr, and refused: a decoder must not accept what its
      own encoder cannot produce, the same rule as a resume bitfield's
      trailing bits. No interop cost — every implementation derives the label
      as base32(SHA-256(dest)), which always leaves those bits zero — so a
      label with them set was not produced by any conforming client.

1.3 [decided] Inbound / naming: SAM reports a peer's *full* base64
    destination (I2P's base64 alphabet is standard base64 with '+'->'-' and
    '/'->'~'). Its hash is SHA-256 of the decoded destination bytes. These
    compose: base32(SHA-256(dest)) is exactly the b32 label, so a dialed
    peer and an accepted peer reconcile to the same DestHash.

1.4 [decided] Compact tracker peer lists on I2P are concatenated 32-byte
    destination hashes, no port (unlike clearnet's 6-byte ip:port). Non-
    compact responses give full base64 destinations under each peer dict's
    `ip` key. Both parsed in clove-core::tracker.


5. HTTP tracker announces (clove-core::tracker, M3)
---------------------------------------------------

5.1 [assumed] An announce is an HTTP/1.1 GET to the tracker's I2P
    destination. clove sends the standard BEP 3 query params plus:
      - `compact=1` (we require the 32-byte-hash compact form, §1.4);
      - `port=6881` as a placeholder — I2P has no port, but trackers/params
        parsers expect the key;
      - `ip=<our base64 destination>` — the I2P convention for telling the
        tracker our destination, since there is no IP to observe.

    [decided] as of 2026-07-27, and now against the normative spec rather than
    by inference. The I2P BitTorrent specification (i2p.net, "Bittorrent over
    I2P") states it plainly: *"The value of the ip key is the base 64 of the
    client's Destination"*, that *"current Destinations for clients are 387 or
    more bytes (516 or more in Base 64 encoding)"*, and that a tracker
    *"should probably decode and reject bad Base64 when announced"*.

    All three sentences describe what happened to us. The parameter is
    required (omitting it answers `failure reason: Missing key: ip`); the
    value is the 387-plus-byte Destination, not the 679-byte private key blob
    clove was sending; and postman's tracker rejecting the bad value was the
    spec's own recommended behaviour. See §5.1c. This closes the oldest
    [assumed] in this file.

    Observed variant, unconfirmed: XD (`lib/tracker/http.go`) builds `ip` from
    its network address plus a `.i2p` suffix rather than from a base64
    Destination. Whether that resolves to the same thing depends on what its
    SAM layer returns from `Addr()`, which the announce code does not say —
    recorded because a second form may exist in the wild, not as a licence to
    send one.

5.1a [decided] Trackers answer `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, and clove's HTTP
    client did not implement it. postman's `announce.php` does, as any PHP
    script behind an ordinary webserver will. The reader fell through to its
    read-until-close branch and handed the *chunk framing* to the bencode
    parser, which reported "not bencode" — accurate about `1a4\r\n…` and
    useless. Every announce failed identically, so the client acquired no
    peers at all, on any torrent, ever (found live 2026-07-27).

    Decoding is implemented in `clove_core::http` with the same hostile-input
    discipline as the rest: bounded size lines, extensions parsed off and
    ignored, `max_body` checked against the announced chunk size before
    anything is allocated, trailers consumed rather than mistaken for body,
    and chunked overriding a `Content-Length` sent alongside it (RFC 9112
    §6.1). Announces that are *not* bencode now carry a bounded printable
    prefix of what did arrive, so the next surprise is one log line rather
    than a session.

5.1b [decided] `tracker2.postman.i2p` answered an announce with **HTTP 200 and a
    styled HTML page** (observed 2026-07-27, i2pd 2.x, live). Not a bencoded
    `failure reason`, not an error status — a web page, served where an
    announce belongs.

    **RESOLVED to a refusal, 2026-07-27.** With HTML summarised rather than
    quoted, the page names itself:

      HTML page titled "PaTracker 1.7.8 | Blocked Request"; text begins
      "Request denied! Your request was found to be in violation of the
       site's policy. In most cases this is caused by issues related to I2P,
       like incomplete data and such."

    And the endpoint is demonstrably alive: the same URL fetched from a
    browser through the router's SOCKS proxy answers
    `d14:failure reason22:Missing key: info_hashe` — correct bencode for a
    bare GET. So `/announce.php` routes, PaTracker is running, and it is
    **clove's announce specifically** that it refuses.

    That narrows it to the request. The candidates, in the order they are
    worth deleting:
      1. `ip=<base64 destination>` (§5.1, still [assumed]). The tracker sees
         our destination from the stream itself; a client asserting one in a
         parameter is the most likely thing a policy check objects to, and
         "incomplete data related to I2P" reads like a complaint about
         exactly this field.
      2. `port=6881`, a placeholder for a protocol with no ports.
      3. `numwant`, `compact`, `event` — ordinary, but untested here.

    Do not reason about which; delete them one at a time. The daemon now logs
    the literal URL it sent on every failed announce, so the test is to paste
    that into the same browser and remove parameters until it stops being
    refused. One round of that is worth more than another round of theory —
    three prior theories about this failure (address book, chunked encoding,
    wrong endpoint) were all wrong, and each cost a live session.

    **It is postman.** The page's own stylesheet declares `--postman:` and
    embeds an SVG of a postman; 14151 bytes, `content-type text/html;
    charset=utf-8`. So the destination is right and the address book is not
    the problem — that candidate is closed. What remains:
      (a) postman's webserver declines the request and serves the site
          instead. Two shapes in clove's announce were unusual enough to be
          worth removing before blaming the endpoint: `Content-Length: 0` on
          a bodyless GET, which almost nothing on the web sends, and **no
          `User-Agent` at all**. Both are fixed; §5.1's `ip=` form remains
          [assumed] and is the next candidate if they were not it.
      (b) `/announce.php` is no longer the announce endpoint, and the server
          answers 200 with its front page for paths it does not route.

    Settle it from outside clove before changing anything else — one request
    through the router's HTTP proxy says which:

        curl -x http://127.0.0.1:4444 -i http://tracker2.postman.i2p/announce.php

    A bencoded `failure reason` means the endpoint is live and clove's request
    is at fault; the same HTML page means the endpoint is (b).

    Two previews were spent learning that a modern page's first 512 bytes are
    its stylesheet. `preview` now summarises HTML — `<title>` plus the first
    visible words — rather than quoting the head, which is what "identify the
    page" actually needs.

5.1c [decided] **SAM's `DESTINATION=` field is the session's private key
    blob, not its destination — and clove was announcing the whole thing.**
    Root cause of every failure in §5.1b, found 2026-07-27 by decoding the
    announce clove actually sent.

    `SESSION STATUS RESULT=OK DESTINATION=<b64>` returns the *private keys*.
    The public destination is only their first 387-or-so bytes; the remainder
    is the private crypto and signing key. Captured from i2pd: 908 base64
    characters, 679 bytes — 391 of destination (a KEY certificate with a
    4-byte payload) and 288 of key material. Both halves are one unbroken
    base64 run, so nothing about the string says where one ends.

    Consequences, all of which had been misread as something else:

      - **Every announce carried our private keys** to the tracker in `ip=`.
        postman's PaTracker refused each one as a policy violation, which was
        correct and generous. The bisect that found it: dropping `ip=`
        entirely changes the answer to `failure reason: Missing key: ip`, so
        the parameter is required *and* ours was wrong.
      - **Our own `DestHash` was the hash of the private blob.** Every b32
        clove printed, published over PEX, or dialled in a loopback test named
        nothing at all. A router asked to resolve it can only fail — which is
        precisely the "lease set not found" that §2.8 recorded as an emissary
        defect, and the `CantReachPeer` that §2.6b blamed on young routers.
        Those entries stand as observations; their attribution was wrong.

    clove now cuts the blob to its destination before using it, at the one
    place it enters the process (`i2pnet::addr::destination_bytes`). The
    length comes from the certificate's own header, so it survives whatever
    key types a router uses. Anything too short to hold a certificate header
    is refused rather than guessed at: a wrong answer here is a wrong
    identity, and a wrong identity fails silently everywhere at once.

    The lesson for the rest of this file: **`§2.8` and `§2.6b` were written
    from live logs and were still wrong**, because the evidence was consistent
    with a router bug and nobody decoded what we were sending. Prefer decoding
    your own output before attributing a fault upstream.

5.2 [decided] Any query already present in the announce URL (e.g. postman's
    `/announce.php?...`) is preserved; clove appends its params with the
    correct `?`/`&` separator.

5.3 [decided] Announce timing: honor the tracker's `interval`, floored at a
    local 60s minimum so a misconfigured tracker cannot make us hammer.
    On failure, exponential backoff from 30s capped at 30 min. A tracker
    `failure reason` is surfaced to the operator, not retried as if
    transient.

5.4 [open] Whether i2psnark/opentracker expect `event=started` on the very
    first announce only, and any tracker-specific quirks (numwant caps,
    `&supportcrypto`, etc.), to be confirmed live at M3 and recorded here.

5.5 [decided] A tracker URL's host is usually an **address-book name**
    (`tracker2.postman.i2p`), not a b32, and the two resolve through entirely
    different machinery: the address book is a local file the router fills from
    HTTP subscriptions, while a b32 resolves through the netDb. A router that
    has not completed its subscription fetch cannot resolve the first form at
    all, however healthy it otherwise is.

    This interacts badly with the negative-lookup backoff (R6,
    `i2pnet::naming`): the hold doubles from 30s to 30 minutes, so a name that
    is unknown at startup is retried *less* often as time passes. The observable
    symptom is therefore not a stream of failed lookups but an absence of
    lookups, which reads from outside like a stalled client.

    Neither behaviour is wrong — caching a miss is the point — but both were
    invisible. The lookup error now states the remaining hold, and
    `try_fetch_round` reports which stage failed rather than dropping it
    (first found live 2026-07-27; see LIVE-TESTING §7a). An operator hitting
    this can sidestep it with a b32 tracker URL.


2. SAM / yosemite behavior (from the 0.7.0 API review, M0/M1)
------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 [decided] yosemite hardcodes the SAM host to 127.0.0.1; only the TCP
    port is configurable. This happens to enforce Layer 1's loopback-only
    rule for free. Consequence: the documented `--i-know-sam-is-remote`
    escape hatch (SCOPE §5) is NOT expressible through the yosemite backend
    as it stands — a remote SAM bridge would need a different backend or an
    upstream change. Noted rather than worked around.

2.2 [decided] yosemite's synchronous Stream exposes `split(self) ->
    (ReadHalf, WriteHalf)`, not a duplicating `try_clone`. clove's
    `I2pStream` trait was corrected to a split-based shape (Phase D) so the
    mock and the SAM backend share one honest abstraction, and it matches
    the reader-thread/writer-thread model (Q5) directly.

2.3 [decided, superseded] clove no longer uses yosemite to dial, and its own
    `STREAM CONNECT` honours the caller's timeout on a socket it owns
    (§2.12). The observation below stands as a fact about yosemite 0.7 and is
    why the trait's `timeout` argument was documented as advisory for so long.

2.3 [assumed] yosemite's `Session::connect` (sync) has no timeout parameter
    and blocks until the STREAM CONNECT resolves. clove's dial() takes a
    timeout for the trait's sake but cannot enforce it at this layer; the
    timeout is a supervision-layer concern. Whether long connects need a
    watchdog is an [open] R2 question for the stress harness.

2.4 [assumed] `Session::connect`/`accept`/`forward` all take `&mut self` and
    block. A single Session shared between the dial path and an accept path
    therefore serializes. clove's SAM backend currently implements only the
    outbound + naming paths on the shared Session (behind a Mutex); the
    inbound topology is deferred (see 2.5).

2.5 [assumed] Inbound streams use STREAM FORWARD, not STREAM ACCEPT. ACCEPT
    is one accept per inbound stream on &mut self and serializes every
    inbound peer on the one session; FORWARD has the router push each inbound
    stream as a fresh TCP connection to a loopback listener we own (an
    allowed Layer-1 IP socket), so inbound concurrency is a plain accept loop,
    not a lock. This matches the "one PRIMARY session + forwarded listener"
    topology in SCOPE §4. Implemented in i2pnet::sam (SamListener /
    ForwardedStream), decision recorded in LIVE-TESTING.md §3.
    With SILENT=false (yosemite's default, set explicitly) the router prepends
    each forwarded connection with the peer's base64 destination line,
    optionally followed by FROM_PORT/TO_PORT params, terminated by '\n'; we
    read up to that newline one byte at a time (so the peer's stream payload
    is left untouched) and derive the DestHash via §1.3. The split/parse logic
    is unit-tested over loopback TCP; the exact forwarded framing and that a
    forwarded peer's derived hash reconciles with its dialed hash are the
    first things confirmed against a live router (LIVE-TESTING.md §6.1, M1).

2.6 [open] R2 — i2pd SAM behavior under many concurrent streams on one
    session is the suspected root of XD-style flakiness. The reconnect
    supervisor (i2pnet::supervisor) is built and unit-tested against a fake
    factory; its behavior against a *real* router mid-restart, and the
    concurrency ceiling of one session, are the harness's job. First live
    observations against i2pd (M1, in progress):

    2.6a [decided, superseded by §2.12] This was true while dials went through
      yosemite; they no longer do, and a dial now takes no session lock at
      all. The engine may initiate peers concurrently. Kept because the
      reasoning below explains why several budgets in the harness are shaped
      the way they are.

    2.6a [decided] Outbound dial *initiation* serializes per session. yosemite's
      sync `connect` takes `&mut self` and blocks until the router resolves the
      stream, so SamSession::dial holds the session mutex for the whole connect.
      Established streams run concurrently (each is an independent socket once
      connected); only the setup step is serial. Consequence for the engine:
      one dial session initiates peers one-at-a-time. Whether that needs
      multiple dial sessions, or is fine because connects are quick once warm,
      is the N-scaling question the harness still has to answer.

    2.6b [assumed] A freshly created transient destination is briefly
      unreachable: a dial to it returns STREAM STATUS RESULT=CANT_REACH_PEER
      (surfaced as Protocol(Router(CantReachPeer))) for the ~seconds it takes
      the destination to build tunnels and publish its leaseSet. This is normal
      warmup, not a fault; the live test and sam-stress retry the dial through
      it, as the real peer-acquisition path must. Distinct from a *cold router*
      (reseed incomplete → no peers → CantReachPeer indefinitely), which is an
      operator/setup problem (see LIVE-TESTING.md troubleshooting).

      Observed 2026-07-26, all three routers, warmup far longer than "~seconds":
      emissary logged `unable to open stream, lease set not found … Timeout`
      every ~26s (a ~21s router-side lookup timeout plus our 5s backoff) for the
      whole run, and i2pd and Java I2P failed the same single dial. All three
      were young and firewalled (i2pd: 15 min uptime, 33% tunnel build success,
      LeaseSets 0). Whether the floor is minutes on a *healthy* router, or those
      routers were simply not ready, is unresolved — the run could not tell,
      because the harness gave the retry loop 90s of a 240s budget (see 2.6d).
      Re-measure on warm routers before treating any number here as real.

    2.6c [open] Two destinations on **one** router may be a harder case than two
      routers, not an easier one. emissary resolves a same-router destination
      through a full netDb lookup and times out, rather than short-circuiting to
      the local leaseSet it already holds. If that is general rather than an
      emissary gap, the loopback topology every tier-2 test uses is testing
      something stricter than the swarm path it stands in for. Worth an upstream
      question; worth a cross-router control before concluding anything from a
      same-router failure.

      Seen twice now (2026-07-26, both runs), with the listener's inbound
      tunnels confirmed built in emissary's own log immediately before the
      dialing session failed to find its leaseSet. The control now exists:
      `CLOVE_SAM_PORT_DIAL` puts the dialer on a different router and `make
      cross` sweeps every ordered pair. Until a cross-router pair has been
      tried, a same-router failure implicates nothing.

    2.6e [decided] SAM `NAMING LOOKUP` of a `.b32.i2p` label is **not** a test of
      leaseSet publication. Asked to resolve a freshly created local destination,
      the three routers disagreed three ways: i2pd `InvalidKey`, Java I2P
      `KeyNotFound` after 3.6s, emissary `KeyNotFound` in 0.0s — and a lookup
      that returns in no time did not consult the network. The SAM spec defines
      NAMING LOOKUP over address-book names and `ME`; b32 support is
      router-dependent and at least one router rejects the form outright.

      sam-stress briefly drew the wrong conclusion from this ("the leaseSet is
      not resolvable … chase publication"). It now reports the router's answer as
      fact and looks up the *dialer's own* destination as a control: both
      failing means the router does not answer b32 lookups for its own session
      destinations and the result says nothing about the target; only divergence
      between the two implicates publication.

    2.6d [decided] The harness's own budgets must derive from one another, not
      sit as independent constants. `make router-ready` set a 90s run budget
      while sam-stress kept a fixed 240s warmup-retry budget, so the probe killed
      every run a third of the way into the retrying it had itself configured,
      then reported the router as "not completing dials". Every router failed
      there and the whole matrix skipped behind it. The retry window is now
      computed from the run budget (`warmup_deadline`), with a regression test;
      READY_DEADLINE is 240s.

      That fix was then reintroduced one layer up: `ci/live-report.sh` wrapped
      the probe in a bare `timeout 180` while READY_DEADLINE was 240, so all
      three routers reported TIMEOUT instead of a result. Making a rule
      unrepresentable in one language does nothing about the copy of the number
      in another. Every nested deadline in the shell now derives from the one it
      contains.

      Related: with dial setup serialized (2.6a), a *fixed* run budget is a
      silent ceiling on N — at N=128 even a 3s connect needs 384s, more than the
      360s default. sam-stress now prints that arithmetic when streams are left
      unfinished, so the reader is not sent to diagnose a router for the
      harness's own limit.

2.7 [decided] yosemite 0.7.0 sets no read or write timeout on its SAM control
    socket. Against a bridge that accepts the TCP connection and then says
    nothing, `Session::new` blocks forever — it does not error, so nothing
    upstream of it can back off, retry, or log. Measured in-tree against a
    fake bridge: of six misbehaviours (close, silence, garbage, endless
    bytes, refuse-hello, one-byte-dribble), four hung indefinitely and only
    the two that closed or answered returned at all.

    clove's answer is a pre-flight probe (i2pnet::sam::probe_bridge): before
    yosemite is handed the port, clove opens its own socket with a connect
    timeout, a per-read timeout, a whole-exchange deadline and a 512-byte cap,
    speaks `HELLO VERSION` itself, and requires `HELLO REPLY RESULT=OK`. All
    six cases now fail cleanly and quickly, with an error naming the address.

2.7a [decided, closed by §2.12] Outbound streams are no longer yosemite's.
    A dialled peer that goes silent is bounded by `I2pStream::set_timeouts`
    like any other socket, and dropping the stream closes it — so the parked
    thread and the leaked socket described below are both gone. The entry is
    kept because it is the reason the socket count climbed all afternoon, and
    because the *inbound* half of its reasoning is still exactly right.

2.7a [was open] The same gap applies to yosemite's *virtual streams*, and there the
    probe trick does not help: `yosemite::Stream` (and the ReadHalf a peer's
    reader thread owns) exposes no read or write timeout, so a dialled peer that
    completes its handshake and then goes silent parks that thread until the
    router tears the stream down. clove drops such a peer from its table on the
    idle timeout (clove_core::torrent, DEFAULT_IDLE_TIMEOUT) so it stops
    counting against the peer cap and its blocks go back to the picker, but the
    thread itself is not reclaimable from here.

    Inbound is unaffected: a forwarded connection is a loopback TCP socket clove
    owns (i2pnet::sam::ForwardedStream), and I2pStream::set_timeouts bounds it —
    which is what the demux uses to stop a connection that never handshakes from
    holding a thread for the life of the process.

    Closing this needs a timeout on yosemite's stream types, the same upstream
    change §2.7 wants for the control socket. Until then the cost is one parked
    thread per silent outbound peer, bounded in practice by how many peers a
    torrent dials.
    Budget is SamConfig::probe_timeout (default 10s).

    Residual gap, deliberately not papered over: a bridge that answers HELLO
    correctly and *then* stalls on SESSION CREATE still hangs inside
    yosemite. The probe cannot see that far. Closing it needs either read
    timeouts upstream in yosemite, or proxying yosemite's control connection
    through a socket clove owns so it can be shut down — the latter is real
    complexity for a failure mode much rarer than "the router is not up yet",
    so it waits for evidence from live running. Tracked by the test
    `a_bridge_that_passes_hello_and_then_stalls_is_a_known_gap`.

2.8 [decided] emissary 0.4.0, first live run (2026-07-25): every dial between two
    destinations on the *same* emissary router failed. The router's own log is
    unambiguous about why:

      WARN emissary::sam::session: unable to open stream, lease set not found
           session_id="clove-stress-dial" destination_id=uLFobsL8 error=Timeout

    Not CANT_REACH_PEER from a failed tunnel build: the dialing session cannot
    resolve the *leaseSet* of a destination hosted by the very router it is
    talking to. The target session's tunnels were up at the time — the same log
    shows "inbound tunnel built name=clove-stress-listen" — so this is a netDb
    lookup that goes to the network and times out, rather than being answered
    locally. 19 such timeouts across the run; zero dials succeeded.

    Also seen, immediately after each of that session's inbound tunnel builds:

      WARN emissary::tunnel::pool: unhandled message name=clove-stress-listen
           message_type=DeliveryStatus

    DeliveryStatus is how a netDb store is acknowledged, so an unhandled one is
    consistent with the leaseSet never being recorded as published.

    Two candidate explanations, not yet separated:
      (a) emissary does not short-circuit leaseSet lookups for destinations it
          hosts itself, so a same-router dial needs a full floodfill round trip
          — which a young router with a thin netDb cannot complete. Java I2P
          and i2pd both answer locally, which is why the loopback topology
          (LIVE-TESTING §5.2) was assumed cheap.
      (b) the router was simply too young (~30 min, first boot) and would
          succeed given hours.

    RESOLVED in favour of (a) by the second run (2026-07-25, router up ~1h):
    same failure, and the router was demonstrably healthy at the time —

      INFO emissary::router: taking backup of profile storage num_routers=327
      INFO emissary::netdb: publish router info router_id=vfjZfWJx
           floodfills=["JsQjTcor", "JpP9fT5x", "JRKwxnGk"]

    327 known routers, three floodfills, client tunnel pools building inbound
    and outbound throughout. Not a cold router. Every lookup for a leaseSet
    belonging to a destination *on this same router* still timed out. Age is
    not the variable; the local lookup is.

    Worth reporting upstream with this log. The workaround, if one is needed
    before then, is for the loopback test to use two routers rather than two
    destinations on one — which costs the M1 criterion its simplicity, so it
    waits until upstream has had a chance to look.

    Nothing here implicates clove: the dial is an ordinary SAM STREAM CONNECT
    to a b32, and the router refuses it before any of our code is involved. It
    is recorded because SCOPE §6 predicted exactly this ("young router, young
    SAM impl — expect to find bugs on both sides; coordinate upstream"), and
    because the same *symptom* (CantReachPeer) appeared earlier against i2pd
    without a log detailed enough to say whether the cause was the same.

2.9 [decided] SAM session ids are per-router and are not necessarily released
    when the control socket closes. emissary 0.4.0 holds one long enough that
    a process starting seconds after a clean exit is refused:

      WARN emissary::sam: duplicate session id session_id=clove-stress-listen

    Observed as three consecutive sam-stress runs (N=32, 64, 128) failing at
    session setup with `DuplicateId`, immediately after the N=16 run had
    exited normally. The runs were seconds apart, which is exactly the shape
    of an automated matrix sweep.

    clove now derives every session id from a base name plus its pid and a
    sub-second component (`i2pnet::sam::unique_nickname`). The id is not the
    identity — that is the destination key (Q4) — so it can vary per process
    freely, and it stays readable enough to find in a router console.

    This is not only a harness concern. The SCOPE §4 reconnect discipline has
    cloved rebuild its session tree after losing the router; with a fixed id,
    that rebuild can be refused by the stale session it is replacing, and the
    supervisor backs off and retries into the same refusal indefinitely — the
    precise failure it exists to prevent. cloved uses a unique id per attempt
    for that reason.

2.10 [open] A SAM bridge answering `HELLO` does not mean the router can carry
    a stream. Java I2P 2.12.0 accepted the TCP connection and completed the
    pre-flight handshake (§2.7) ~25s after container start, then reset the
    connection on `SESSION CREATE`:

      seeder SAM session: Os { code: 104, kind: ConnectionReset }

    The bridge binds early in the router's startup; the router behind it was
    still coming up. Port-open and HELLO-OK are both necessary and neither is
    sufficient, so `make router-ready` (two sessions, one dial, bounded) is
    the gate worth trusting before a live tier, and the report now runs it
    first rather than spending five minutes per stress level to learn the
    same thing. How long Java I2P actually needs before SESSION CREATE
    succeeds is still unmeasured.


2.11 [decided] The loopback topology every tier-2 test uses is *harder* on the
    router than the swarm path it stands in for, and it was gating the swarm
    path. Recorded because three live sessions were spent on the strict side of
    that asymmetry without any of them saying anything about clove.

    What the loopback test needs, and the swarm path does not:

      - a destination created seconds ago to have published its leaseSet, and
      - a *second session on the same router* to resolve that leaseSet.

    Both are netDb operations on the freshest possible data. The second one is
    the one emissary cannot do at all with a demonstrably healthy router (2.8),
    and the first is what a young or firewalled router is worst at (2.6b).

    What a swarm peer does instead: resolve a destination that has been
    published for months by a router that wants to be found. And for the
    download half, our own destination need not be resolvable by anyone —
    I2P bundles the sender's leaseSet with the stream's opening message, so the
    far side answers without a netDb lookup. A leecher that no floodfill has
    ever heard of still completes a download.

    Consequence for the test suite, not for the wire: run the swarm tier first
    (`make swarm`, ci/live-swarm.sh), and treat a loopback failure alongside
    swarm success as a finding about the router. The loopback tier stays — it
    is a fine diagnostic, and sam-stress is still the only instrument that
    answers R2 — but it is no longer the gate, and ci/live-report.sh no longer
    skips everything behind it.

    Note the direction of the remaining gap: *seeding* does need our leaseSet
    published and resolvable by a remote router, since that is how a peer
    reaches us at all. That is exactly what the `inbound-peer` milestone
    measures (clove_core::torrent::Torrent::inbound_peers), and it is a
    stronger result than the loopback test's inbound leg, because the router
    that resolved us is somebody else's and had its own reasons.

2.12 [decided] yosemite 0.7.0's session controller is **one state machine
    shared by the control connection and every stream operation**, and a
    stream failure on an unexpected path leaves it unusable for the life of
    the session. Found live 2026-07-27, from a run that spent ten minutes
    cycling between `downloading` and `waiting-for-router` with zero peers.

    Every entry point (`handshake_stream`, `create_stream`, `handle_response`)
    opens with `mem::replace(&mut self.state, SessionState::Poisoned)` and
    restores the state only on the paths it expects. So:

      - `STREAM STATUS RESULT=<error>` — **recovered.** yosemite explicitly
        resets `stream_state` to `Uninitialized` here, so an unreachable peer
        (`CANT_REACH_PEER`) costs one dial and nothing else. This is the
        common case on I2P and it is handled correctly.
      - an unparseable or unexpected reply to the stream `HELLO`
        (`InvalidMessage` / `InvalidState`), or a write/read failing partway
        through `connect` — **not recovered.** The controller stays `Poisoned`
        or mid-handshake, and every later `connect` on that session returns
        `InvalidState`. Permanently. The state is private, so nothing outside
        yosemite can reset it.

    Observed signature, repeating on a ~90–120s cycle: one operation fails
    with `protocol error: invalid message from router`, the next with
    `protocol error: invalid state`, and then the session is rebuilt and it
    starts over. `healthy()` did not catch it, because `send_command` writes a
    `PING` on the control socket without consulting the state machine — a
    wedged session answers PING perfectly happily while refusing every dial.

    **FIXED 2026-07-27 by not using yosemite for dials at all.** SAMv3 does
    not require a shared controller: a stream is its own connection. clove now
    opens its own socket to the bridge per dial, speaks `HELLO VERSION` then
    `STREAM CONNECT` on it, and the socket it is holding *is* the stream
    (`i2pnet::sam::dial_stream`). Nothing is shared, so nothing can be
    poisoned by another dial's failure. XD does the same thing and cannot have
    this bug for the same reason.

    Three other entries close with it, all of which were consequences of not
    owning the socket:

      - **§2.3** — the dial timeout is real. It now bounds the wait for
        `STREAM STATUS`, which is where a leaseSet lookup spends its time.
      - **§2.7a** — a dialled peer that goes silent no longer parks a thread
        and leaks a socket for the life of the process. An outbound stream is
        a `ForwardedStream` like an inbound one: `set_timeouts` works on it,
        and dropping it closes it. This was measurable as a socket count
        against the bridge that climbed all afternoon.
      - **§2.6a** — dial setup no longer serializes per session. The
        serialization point was yosemite's `connect(&mut self)` and the
        session mutex clove needed around it; a dial now takes no session
        lock at all, consulting it only for the nickname.

    The mitigation this replaces is deleted rather than kept: the `wedged`
    flag, its classification function and the supervisor's two-way teardown
    message are gone, because nothing can set them any more. So is
    `SamStream`, the yosemite-backed stream type, which nothing constructs.

    yosemite keeps what it is good at and what happens once: `SESSION CREATE`,
    `STREAM FORWARD`, `NAMING LOOKUP`. Its controller can still be poisoned in
    principle, but only by an operation that runs at setup, where the failure
    is a failed session bring-up and the supervisor's ordinary retry covers
    it. Worth reporting upstream regardless (R1) — the next consumer will hit
    it, and a state machine that poisons itself on an unexpected reply is a
    defect whether or not clove still depends on it.

3. Reconnect discipline (i2pnet::supervisor, M1)
------------------------------------------------

3.1 [decided] On session loss the whole session tree is rebuilt on an
    exponential backoff (default 1s doubling to 60s) with jitter (default
    0.2) to avoid a thundering-herd reconnect across many torrents. Torrents
    see a visible "waiting for router" state, never a hard failure.

3.2 [decided] A healthy session that dies once retries promptly: only failed
    *rebuilds* grow the backoff counter, not the loss itself. This keeps a
    one-off router blip from imposing a long stall.


4. Wire protocol (clove-core::wire, M2)
---------------------------------------

4.1 [decided] The peer wire protocol above the I2P stream is byte-identical
    to clearnet BEP 3 — no ports appear in any message; the peer is an I2P
    destination at the layer below. BEP 6 (fast) and BEP 10 (extended) are
    fully framed by the codec.

4.2 [decided] Block size is fixed at 16 KiB (BLOCK_LEN); requests and piece
    blocks over that are rejected. Matches i2psnark.

4.3 [decided] i2p_pex (clove-core::pex): a bencoded dict with `added` and
    `dropped` byte strings, each concatenated 32-byte destination hashes. No
    IPv6/`added6` keys. A message over MAX_PEX_PEERS (512) is rejected as
    spam.

    **Correction, 2026-07-27.** This entry previously asserted that "unlike
    clearnet ut_pex there are no `added.f` flag bytes". That is wrong: the
    I2P specification says the dict has **up to three keys — `added`,
    `added.f` and `dropped`** — and that i2p_pex is based on ut_pex, whose
    flag semantics are documented nowhere except libtorrent's source (which
    is what R4 has always said).

    No interop cost, by luck rather than judgement: `PexMessage::parse` takes
    the keys it knows and ignores the rest, so an `added.f` from i2psnark is
    dropped rather than refused. clove neither sends nor honours flags, which
    stays true until there is a reason. But the assertion was made without
    checking, sat in a file whose whole purpose is to be checkable, and was
    corrected only when somebody read the spec — the same failure as §5.1c,
    one layer up. [open] remains: what the flags mean and whether any of them
    should change behaviour.

    Whether PEX is working at all is now countable rather than inferable:
    Torrent::pex_learned counts destinations first heard of in a peer's PEX
    message, and the daemon publishes it as `pex_peers`. known_peers grows from
    three sources at once, so watching that number climb during a live run
    proved nothing about which source was responsible — which is why M3's "PEX
    acquisition observed" box sat unticked with no way to tick it honestly.

4.4 [decided] ut_metadata (BEP 9, clove-core::metadata): standard 16 KiB
    metadata pieces; a data message is a bencoded {msg_type,piece,
    total_size} header immediately followed by the raw piece bytes.
    Reassembled bytes are SHA-1-verified against the info-hash before use,
    so a lying peer cannot substitute a different torrent. `total_size`
    over 8 MiB is refused up front. Extension ids: clove advertises
    i2p_pex=1, ut_metadata=2 and replies to a peer using the ids that peer
    advertised (peers reply to us using ours). fetch_metadata drives the
    magnet bootstrap: BT+extension handshake, request all pieces, verify,
    then MetaInfo::from_info_dict. Verified in-tree over the mock (a client
    fetches an info dict from a serving instance).

4.5 [decided] Magnet links (clove-core::magnet): `magnet:?xt=urn:btih:<hex
    40 | base32 32>`; `dn` and `tr` are percent-decoded, `tr` filtered to
    I2P-only like .torrent announce URLs.

4.6 [open] I2P `maggot://` links are a distinct, underspecified format.
    Not parsed yet — we will not guess a grammar we cannot verify against
    real examples. Add support (and the grammar) here once confirmed.
